#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538018} :activity/id #uuid "716c0ba1-fccc-5764-a438-73e0b60d1986" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048786 :topic/id #uuid "bea5c820-dd0e-5c3b-936d-578694b34907" :topic/math-academy-id 4171} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48adeec5-fd51-5fd0-9cbb-865947abcfc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432346} {:answer/id #uuid "603d35dd-8efa-5544-b92b-f29e859ccbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432348} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fee454-4dd2-565f-bb9f-e0034cd35e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432356} {:answer/id #uuid "c0760b03-d85d-5f8d-87d7-29831c845c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432358} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bba0dc-736f-564b-a952-443458426144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432358} :answer-field/id #uuid "d001a817-7196-53c1-ab12-d39da924742e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d7c182a-d8d9-5d67-83bd-5c70deb44da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162947" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}+{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & ∗ \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3} & {x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}+2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}6xy & 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & 6xy+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-1)+3+(x-1)=2x+1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4b34b923-8f68-50a9-bb52-bc00feede7d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538055}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2b6bcc1-8db6-5e4c-b7a4-47335b00fb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367903} {:answer/id #uuid "de6fddc6-e1b9-5d30-9c0c-893cee0b8134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367904} {:answer/id #uuid "49cb1040-19f7-5f49-9ce0-3dd45d1ac089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367905} {:answer/id #uuid "6e90e2a3-bb21-5559-a137-484d7df7a4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367906} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d2a724-2946-56a5-bbcd-b914bdaf0ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367904} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe8cfa7-31d7-5120-9151-09adc2f21518" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "34533486-cd0d-5205-b2b8-1ba449843c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-164861" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\pi ,1,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\pi ,1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(\\pi ,1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(\\pi ,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\pi  \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\pi  \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\pi  \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\pi ,0,1) & ≈-1+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 0-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 0-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 0-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+1+\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a161e24e-4b2d-58e4-90bb-731fdf5cfcd0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a5e6d9-c4fd-57bb-8147-cf98861b9d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367813} {:answer/id #uuid "1719c348-9c4f-5f66-9ae0-a4eafaef1b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367814} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f81270-21c7-5914-a3c9-3982421e3eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367815} {:answer/id #uuid "f5eb386a-91a6-5494-94a1-33f62862e3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367816} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb293b3-3530-5187-923a-dc9dda0e6dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367817} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4e6984-cf2e-55cb-9a10-debfbaccb6f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cdc26b29-0619-5b64-aa82-450f2b706603" :question/math-academy-id "q-163106" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y} \\\\ -{e}^{x-y} & {e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-1)+(-1)+(x-1)=2x-3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c692cc95-8166-5828-bb6e-ded3809b88d6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125eb685-2a0e-5fa5-8eef-2a5c11bafde4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281865} {:answer/id #uuid "41249f9a-6302-5f81-b2fb-b1233590ee7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282248} {:answer/id #uuid "8c267805-6601-53a4-b95b-3437c56bc43f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282901} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cf3021-a1f2-5d0b-9147-6fdea11642ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283154} {:answer/id #uuid "f248704e-e6ff-5bdc-b503-211e1cb8e600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283154} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fed9658-24a4-51e8-84a8-b1b99db53222" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6aa91597-6ee2-53be-bdf3-8769a0188799" :question/math-academy-id "q-164858" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,1) & =1, & \\,{f}^{′}(0,1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(0,1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2,1) & ≈1+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1+1+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2}(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ea8a7b8a-eeda-579f-a15b-58bb06b58b03" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7d3174-3459-579a-b643-b960a7dd075c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520008} {:answer/id #uuid "53608405-55af-52ab-8bb1-8ba8739d1150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520117} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9e8b65-e8ee-589b-9251-9db47e2725f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520136} {:answer/id #uuid "8572f2aa-9106-5587-bca8-e37536bbe74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520184} {:answer/id #uuid "cd348c35-901b-57a1-93a1-95ed5535c117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520117} :answer-field/id #uuid "be0946b1-502a-5aba-b006-539568a4bc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "983968fc-15f6-5756-ab92-7399b778fa93" :question/math-academy-id "q-163108" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{e}^{y}),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax+by-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+cxy.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{y}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x+{e}^{y}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{x{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x+1y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-1)xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-1)=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb8b1e7d-342f-57bb-bd13-798188236e53" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538064}}] 